<%# HALF ONE: THE PHOTOGRAPHS WE FOUND.

    Two sections, and the second one is the point. "In the model" is the set the
    identity was built from; "Found but not used" is every candidate we passed over,
    with the reason. A gallery of winners alone cannot answer the question the
    operator is actually asking — is the search any good? — because a search that
    returned twenty watermarked thumbnails and one usable portrait shows the same
    single winner as a search that returned twenty good portraits we capped. %>
<% chosen = @gallery.select(&:chosen?) %>
<% rejected = @gallery.reject(&:chosen?) %>

<div data-test="reference-input-panel" class="card p-4 sm:p-5 space-y-4">
  <header class="space-y-1">
    <p class="label-upper text-primary">1 &middot; Input</p>
    <h2 class="text-lg font-bold text-heading">Reference photos</h2>
    <p class="text-xs text-muted">
      <%= pluralize(chosen.length, "photo") %> going into the model<%= ", #{rejected.length} found and passed over" if rejected.any? %>.
    </p>
    <%# THE TWO GENERATORS DO NOT GET THE SAME SET, and one count would describe whichever
        one the reader happened to be thinking about.

        THREE STATES, NOT TWO, and conflating the last two is a count that lies. A chosen
        photograph either goes to both generators, or to the character sheet only (nothing
        measured its face SIZE, or it is under the trainer's floor; size is what Higgsfield's prepare step turns on — four of
        six measured mints failed there), or to NEITHER (nothing looked at it at all, so
        nothing can say whose face it is). The third state is a row an older ranking chose;
        a line that folded it into the second would claim the sheet uses a photograph the
        sheet refuses. Each renders only when it has members. %>
    <% sheet_only = chosen.select { |p| p.reference_eligible?(@person.full_name) } %>
    <% sheet_only = sheet_only.reject { |p| p.mint_eligible?(@person.full_name) } %>
    <% offered_to_neither = chosen.reject { |p| p.reference_eligible?(@person.full_name) } %>
    <% if sheet_only.any? %>
      <p data-test="trainer-subset" class="text-2xs text-warning-ink">
        <%= sheet_only.length %> of these <%= chosen.length %>
        <%= sheet_only.length == 1 ? "is" : "are" %> used by the character sheet but NOT
        offered to Higgsfield&rsquo;s trainer: its face size was not measured or is below
        the trainer&rsquo;s <%= (Appearances::ReferenceEligibility::MINT_FACE_FILL * 100).round %>%
        floor, and four of six measured mints failed at prepare on that variable. The reason
        is on each tile.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <% if offered_to_neither.any? %>
      <p data-test="offered-to-neither" class="text-2xs text-danger-ink">
        <%= pluralize(offered_to_neither.length, "photo") %> here
        <%= offered_to_neither.length == 1 ? "was" : "were" %> picked by an earlier ranking
        and <%= offered_to_neither.length == 1 ? "is" : "are" %> now offered to NEITHER
        generator. Re-run the search to re-judge
        <%= offered_to_neither.length == 1 ? "it" : "them" %>.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </header>

  <%# THE SEARCH CONTROL. Until the keyless Wikimedia Commons provider shipped, the
      UNCONFIGURED branch was the one that ran on every machine, and this comment said
      so; now a provider is always available and the configured branch is the normal
      one. The unconfigured copy still names the environment variable, because whoever
      reads it is whoever would buy a paid provider.

      BOTH BRANCHES CARRY A data-test, and that is a lesson rather than a flourish.
      Only the `else` had one, so when the keyless provider flipped this condition an
      e2e spec asserting "search is off" had nothing to move ITS assertion to — it just
      went red with no positive state to name. A branch nobody expects to render is
      exactly the one that will. %>
  <div class="rounded-lg border border-subtle bg-surface-alt p-3 space-y-2">
    <% if @search_available %>
      <div data-test="search-configured" data-provider="<%= @search_provider %>"
           class="flex items-start gap-2 flex-wrap justify-between">
        <div class="min-w-0 space-y-0.5">
          <p class="text-xs text-heading font-bold">
            Web image search &mdash; <span class="font-mono"><%= @search_provider %></span>
          </p>
          <p class="text-2xs text-muted break-words">
            <%# WHAT A SEARCH ACTUALLY COSTS depends on which provider is serving, and
                saying "costs money" flatly was true only while the paid one was the only
                one. Wikimedia Commons bills nothing per query; the vision classifier
                bills per image either way, when it is configured at all. %>
            <%# ⚠ THE COUNT IS NAMED BECAUSE THE BUTTON'S PRICE CHANGED. A search now runs
                one query per Appearances::GatherReferencePhotos::QUERY_VARIANTS entry —
                the subject below plus &ldquo;smiling&rdquo;, &ldquo;no helmet&rdquo; and
                &ldquo;laughing&rdquo; — and a control that said "one query" over four
                purchases is the kind of stale cost claim an operator budgets against.
                The queries themselves are listed on the scouting page, which is the
                surface for judging them; this page owes the price. %>
            Will run <%= pluralize(@search_query_count, "search") %> for
            &ldquo;<%= @search_query %>&rdquo; and its expression variants.
            <% if @search_provider == Appearances::ImageSearch::WikimediaCommons.provider_name %>
              The queries themselves are free; ranking the results costs money when a face
              classifier is configured.
            <% else %>
              Each of the <%= @search_query_count %> searches costs money.
            <% end %>
          </p>
        </div>
        <%# ADMINS ONLY, matching the controller. #show is public, so without this
            guard the page offers every visitor a button that buys a query and a
            dozen classifications, and the only thing between them and the purchase
            is a redirect they never asked for. A control nobody but an admin may
            use is furniture on everyone else's page. %>
        <% if admin? %>
          <%= button_to "Search the web",
                search_person_appearance_path(@person.slug, @appearance.slug),
                method: :post, class: "btn btn-sm btn-primary shrink-0",
                form: { data: { test: "search-button" } } %>
        <% end %>
      </div>
    <% else %>
      <div data-test="search-unconfigured" class="space-y-1">
        <p class="text-xs text-heading font-bold">Web image search is off</p>
        <%# THIS BRANCH USED TO BE THE ONE THAT RAN EVERYWHERE, and now it should reach
            nobody: Appearances::ImageSearch::WikimediaCommons needs no credential and
            answers `available?` true on every machine. So the honest message is no
            longer "go and buy a key" — it is "something removed the keyless floor",
            which is a registry edit or a stub rather than a missing purchase. The
            credential is still named because a paid provider is the upgrade, and
            whoever reads this is who would buy it. %>
        <p class="text-2xs text-muted break-words">
          No provider is available, so the photos below are the ones we already had:
          our own mirrored headshot, plus any URL you typed on the model. The keyless
          Wikimedia Commons provider normally covers this, so its absence means the
          provider registry was changed. Set
          <span class="font-mono text-warning-ink"><%= Appearances::ImageSearch::Serper::API_KEY_ENV %></span>
          for a paid provider with better relevance.
        </p>
      </div>
    <% end %>
  </div>

  <% if chosen.any? %>
    <div class="space-y-2">
      <div class="flex items-baseline gap-2 flex-wrap">
        <p class="label-upper">In the model (<%= chosen.length %>)</p>
        <%# WHAT PUT THEM IN THIS ORDER. A helmet hides exactly the features a
            character model is built from, so the ranking is the feature — and an
            operator who cannot see WHICH ranking ran cannot tell a working
            classifier from a silent fallback to shape-and-relevance. %>
        <span data-test="ranking-basis" data-basis="<%= @face_ranked ? "face" : "merit" %>"
              class="text-3xs text-muted">
          <%= @face_ranked ? "ranked by how clearly the face shows" : "ranked on shape and relevance — no face check yet" %>
        </span>
      </div>
      <%# FOUR ACROSS AT DESKTOP because CHOSEN_LIMIT is 6 plus a two-row floor:
          at three across a full set wraps to a ragged third row and leaves the panel
          a head taller than the one beside it. Two across on a phone keeps the
          source and verdict chips on one line each instead of wrapping every tile. %>
      <div data-test="chosen-gallery" class="grid grid-cols-2 sm:grid-cols-3 lg:grid-cols-4 gap-2">
        <%# person_name IS PASSED HERE TOO, though this page shows no calibration block:
            it is what lets a tile say WHICH name it read instead of only "not this
            person". The partial reads it through local_assigns, so passing it is safe. %>
        <%= render partial: "appearances/reference_photo", collection: chosen, as: :photo,
                   locals: { person_name: @person.full_name } %>
      </div>
    </div>
  <% else %>
    <%# NO PHOTOGRAPHS AT ALL is a real state and not an error: a person with no
        athlete record and no typed URL has nothing to build an identity from, and
        Appearances::CreateCharacterReference raises rather than minting from
        nothing. Saying so here is what stops the Request button below reading as
        broken. %>
    <div data-test="no-photos" class="rounded-lg border border-warning/40 bg-warning/10 p-3">
      <p class="text-xs text-warning-ink">
        No photos on file for <%= @person.full_name %>. There is no mirrored headshot
        and no reference URL on this model, so there is nothing to build a character
        model from yet.
      </p>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <% if rejected.any? %>
    <div class="space-y-2">
      <p class="label-upper">Found but not used (<%= rejected.length %>)</p>
      <p class="text-2xs text-muted">
        Kept on purpose. These are how you judge the search rather than only its
        result — including the ones passed over because the face was not visible.
      </p>
      <%# DENSER THAN THE CHOSEN GRID at every size above a phone: these are
          secondary and there can be many of them. Still TWO across on a phone —
          three fit, but the caption chips then wrap to three lines each and the
          reject list becomes the tallest thing on the page. %>
      <div data-test="rejected-gallery" class="grid grid-cols-2 sm:grid-cols-4 lg:grid-cols-5 gap-2">
        <%= render partial: "appearances/reference_photo", collection: rejected, as: :photo,
                   locals: { person_name: @person.full_name } %>
      </div>
    </div>
  <% end %>
</div>
